        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(auto-reply): keep export tree truncation UTF-16 safe",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe exported session HTML tree preview truncated node text with a raw `String.slice(0, maxLen)`. When the cut landed inside an emoji or other supplementary-plane character, the exported tree could render a split surrogate in user-visible preview text.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe final implementation updates the template helper to truncate on code-point boundaries while keeping the existing preview budget and `...` suffix behavior. The regression test exercises a tree-node preview whose truncation boundary crosses an emoji and asserts the rendered tree content stays well-formed.\n\n## User Impact\n\n`/export-session` HTML previews keep truncated message text readable and well-formed when the source content includes emoji or other astral characters near the preview cutoff. A complete pair at the boundary remains intact instead of rendering a broken surrogate half.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Exact reviewed head: `b0ff62cb191485e49bd2369f3d0eaee671a28c2f`\n- `git diff --check`: pass\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/auto-reply/reply/export-html/template.security.test.ts`: pass (16 tests)\n- Regression coverage verifies the tree-node truncation path on an emoji boundary and keeps the surrounding export-html security coverage green\n\nKnown proof gap: this change was validated through the focused export-html Vitest suite only; no broader remote Testbox/Crabbox proof was available in this environment because the local Crabbox binary failed basic sanity checks.\r\n",
